WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.898
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.898 --> 00:00:04.440
SPEAKER 1: Cloud
IDS detects malware,

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.330
spyware, command
and control attacks,

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.189
and other network-based threats.

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:10.380
Its security efficacy
is industry-leading,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.290
built with Palo Alto
Network technology.

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:15.840
Being cloud native, Cloud
IDS is simple to deploy,

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.200
manage, and provides
high performance.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.700
Customers can also use Cloud
IDS to support their compliance

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:21.930
goals.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:24.060
This gives you the
ability to detect exploit

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.813
attempts, port scans,
buffer overflows, and more,

00:00:26.813 --> 00:00:28.980
without having to manage
the infrastructure required

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:30.433
to host an advanced IDS.

00:00:30.433 --> 00:00:32.850
All of this is automatically
integrated with cloud log-in,

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:34.830
allowing for alerts
in UI and the ability

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:37.140
to integrate your
SimSort platform in order

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.810
to take appropriate next
steps for your organization.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:41.970
At a high level,
here's what happens.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:45.210
First, we specify a Cloud IDS
endpoint to direct traffic

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.700
to VMs hosting the IDS system.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.160
Then, in order to analyze
potential threats,

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.870
we set up a packet
mirroring policy

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.782
to duplicate and forward
packets into our IDS endpoint.

00:00:54.782 --> 00:00:56.490
There's a lot of
flexibility around which

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.190
packets get mirrored, as users
can specify to forward packets

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.980
from one or more subnets,
instances with specific network

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.980
tags, or even select
instances by name.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.100
The IDS VMs are then responsible
for analyzing and managing

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.320
the threats that
come into the system.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.900
If threats are detected, they
are logged into Cloud Logging,

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.750
and we can, if we choose,
use Bitquery or other tools

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:19.800
to take automatic actions based
on the threats IDS discovered.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.540
Getting started is
straightforward.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.870
In the UI, within your VPC,
or Virtual Private Cloud,

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:27.390
navigate to the Network
Security Cloud IDS page

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:30.270
and create a new IDS endpoint.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:32.760
We'll give it a name, select
a region for it to operate in,

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.070
and on the next page, we'll
select the threat levels

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.860
we want to be alerted for.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.840
Once we select Create,
behind the scenes,

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.540
Google Cloud will set up
VMs to host the IDS system.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.030
It will set up a load balancer
to manage traffic into it

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:46.830
and a bunch of other
useful infrastructure

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:49.440
to support securing
your platform.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.720
Once that's finished, which
will take about 20 minutes

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.190
at the time of
this video, you'll

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:54.880
attach a new policy
to the endpoint

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.450
to define which package
you want to be mirrored.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.640
In this case, we'll name
the policy, enable it,

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.890
and we'll specify
a subnet and we'll

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:03.443
mirror all packets from it.

00:02:03.443 --> 00:02:04.860
This means that
any traffic coming

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.500
into or out of this range of
IP addresses will be duplicated

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.830
and forward over to the IDS
endpoint we set up previously.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.380
To test this out, over
in the command line

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.070
we'll use Curl to send test
malware attacks to our server.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.200
Back in our Cloud
IDS UI, you'll see

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.600
we now have a few threats
of various levels populated

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.100
in our UI.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.230
By clicking on a threat,
I can see more information

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:27.960
about it, such as the source
and destination IP addresses,

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.438
and more.

00:02:29.438 --> 00:02:31.230
Additionally, you can
click View All Threat

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:34.590
Logs, which will open up a
new tab with the threat logs.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:36.270
At this point, we
could manually take

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:37.860
steps to fix some
of these threats,

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.140
or, just like with any
Cloud Logging logs,

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.320
we could use Bitquery or Pub/Sub
to forward these logs to a SIM

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:46.320
to perform additional
security response steps.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.420
The last thing I want
to show before we go

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:51.360
is the Cloud IDS dashboard
that will show a high level

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.490
overview of your system,
including the top five

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:57.013
threats, source addresses,
and destination IP addresses.

00:02:57.013 --> 00:02:58.680
You can click on any
of these dashboards

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.900
to get more information.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:03.080
This is the power of
Cloud IDS in a nutshell.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.580
It allows you to integrate the
functionality of Google Cloud

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.680
with Palo Alto
Network's advanced IDS,

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.420
giving you a managed
cloud scale security

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.250
system that just works.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.800
For more information about
this and other security topics,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.842
be sure to check out the
links in the description

00:03:15.842 --> 00:03:19.050
for more information on securing
your cloud using Cloud IDS.

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:22.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

